"af_bella|It\u2019s your day off! I booked us a ferry up the coast to the city of Varrow, because there\u2019s a memorial garden here I think you should see.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_adam|It\u2019s your day off! I booked us a ferry up the coast to the city of Varrow, because there\u2019s a memorial garden here I think you should see.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|Welcome to the Lantern Garden. I\u2019m Gus. I take care of it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|Ten years ago, in 2030, a new germ spread through Varrow. We call that year the Quiet Year.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|Back then, Varrow had none of the safeguards you\u2019ll see tonight. Nothing tested the water for new germs, nothing cleaned the air on buses, and nobody checked DNA orders.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|By the time anyone noticed the germ, it was in more than a hundred homes. The only thing left to do was for families to stay home for months, so it couldn\u2019t spread.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|Not everyone. Some people got very sick, and we still miss the ones we lost. But the city pulled through, together.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|It was made with help from an unsafe AI that would do whatever anyone asked. That\u2019s why countries around the world changed their rules about AI and DNA afterward.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|People put a light in the window to say, \u201cWe\u2019re okay, and we\u2019re thinking of you.\u201d The lights stayed in the windows all through the Quiet Year.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|Every lantern here is for one family that helped: nurses, bakers, bus drivers, and kids who stayed home. There are 4,812 of them.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|All of them, because staying home to stop the germ was a way of helping. Varrow had 4,812 families, so the garden has 4,812 lanterns.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|It is. On a still night, you can see the glow from the ferry.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|People left things in the garden from the Quiet Year, and each thing has a story. Will you find them?":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_bella|I\u2019ll listen too. Tap anything that glows!":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_adam|I\u2019ll listen too. Tap anything that glows!":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|Hi, I\u2019m Priya. I built the first water tester, the box you found by the pond.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|People ask me why noticing a germ a few days earlier matters so much. The answer is a kind of math called exponential growth. Come try it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|You\u2019ve seen how fast a germ can grow. Now come see what Varrow built so that a new germ never spreads that far again.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|This is the Safety Table. It shows 100 futures in which someone tries to make a germ like the one in 2030.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|Since then, Varrow has put far-UVC lights in every bus, shop and school. You can\u2019t see the light, but it breaks down germs floating in the air.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|The hospital keeps reusable respirators for every nurse and bus driver, and the schools can fill their halls with a glycol vapor that kills germs in the air.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|We even built clean-air shelters with room for every family. We hope we never need them.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_bella|Each safeguard catches most problems, but none is perfect. Turn them on one at a time and watch how the 100 futures change.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_adam|Each safeguard catches most problems, but none is perfect. Turn them on one at a time and watch how the 100 futures change.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bf_alice|Hi! Gus said you found my drawing. I\u2019m Ivy.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bf_alice|I was seven when I put that first lantern in our window. Now I help hang the new ones.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bf_alice|Every visitor gets to light a lantern. Who would you thank with yours?":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bf_alice|Me too. Staying home was the hardest kind of helping.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bf_alice|Good pick. People hardly ever thank the ones who do quiet jobs like checking orders, but those jobs keep us safe.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bf_alice|That\u2019s okay. Hold the lantern for a minute, and you\u2019ll think of someone.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bf_alice|Here. Pick a color and write your thank-you.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|There. Now there are 4,813 lanterns.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|Most days, nothing happens here: the water testers find nothing new, and the DNA orders pass their checks. Those quiet days are exactly what all the safeguards are for.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|Thank you for listening. Come back anytime. The lanterns will be here.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man_deep|That\u2019s what a memorial is for. When people remember what happened, they keep taking care of the safeguards.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
