"bm_george|germ. A tiny living thing, too small to see, that can make people sick.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|vaccine. A medicine that teaches your body to fight a germ before you ever meet it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|memorial. A place or object that helps people remember something important that happened.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|outbreak. When an illness suddenly spreads to many people in one place.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|screening. Checking things carefully, one by one, to catch the dangerous ones.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|sensor. A device that notices something, like heat, light, or tiny things in the air, and reports it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|safeguard. Something that protects people by stopping a problem before it gets big.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|early warning. A signal that something might be going wrong, sent soon enough to fix it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|wastewater. Used water that drains away from homes, schools and other buildings.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|far-uvc lights. Special lamps whose light breaks down germs floating in the air. At the right strength they are safe for people.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|defense in depth. Using several safeguards stacked together, so if one misses a problem, the next one can catch it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|unsafe ai. An AI built without safety checks, so it would help anyone do anything, even something harmful.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|dna. The instructions inside every living thing, written in a tiny chemical code.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|print shop. A lab that builds DNA to order, the way a print shop prints a poster someone sends in.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|shifts. Blocks of time people work, like a day shift or a night shift.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|flags. Marks something so a person will look at it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|doubles. Becomes twice as big. If 4 homes have the germ and it doubles, 8 homes have it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|exponential growth. Growth that keeps doubling, so it starts slow and then becomes huge very fast.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|respirators. Masks that seal around your face and filter almost every germ out of the air you breathe. Some are made to be cleaned and used again.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|glycol vapor. A very fine mist, harmless to people, that can kill germs floating in the air of a room.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nova|This was my phone in 2030.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nova|Our school closed on a Monday, and I didn\u2019t see my friends in person for five months.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nova|Every night at eight, my family video-called Grandma Luc\u00eda, who lived alone across town.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nova|She taught us her soup recipe over the screen, one step at a time.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nova|When we finally hugged again, she said those calls helped her get through the lonely months.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nova|Staying apart kept us safe, because the germ jumped from person to person when people were close.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_daniel|During the Quiet Year, I closed my bakery\u2019s front door to customers, but I kept the ovens on.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_daniel|Nurses at Varrow General were working very long shifts.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_daniel|So every morning I left bread at the back door, and a delivery robot carried it to the hospital.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_daniel|Neighbors I had never met started leaving bags of flour on my step.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_daniel|By spring, we had fed the whole hospital, one loaf at a time.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|When the germ first reached Varrow, nobody noticed it for almost three weeks.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|By then, the outbreak had already reached more than a hundred homes.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|So after the Quiet Year, my team built this box and named it Sniffer.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|It tests the wastewater that drains out of buildings for new germs, and if it finds one, it sends an alert within a day.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|Today every school and neighborhood in Varrow has a tester like Sniffer, and so does every city and airport in the world.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|An AI watches all of those testers at once, and it alerts the health office the moment something new shows up.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_river|Today, a new germ that reaches Varrow gets noticed within days instead of weeks, while it is still small enough to stop. Varrow\u2019s early warning system is built to catch new germs that early.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_heart|The Quiet Year germ was made with help from an unsafe AI, and its recipe was printed at a DNA print shop.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_heart|After that, the world agreed on a rule: every DNA order gets checked before anything is printed.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_heart|Here in Varrow, an AI checks every order in less than a second.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_heart|Almost every order is for medicine or research, so it prints right away.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_heart|When an order looks odd, the AI flags it, and I decide whether to hold that order until experts can look at it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_heart|Most days, no odd orders turn up at all. I\u2019m glad when that happens, because it means nobody tried to print anything dangerous that day.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_puck|An AI designed the vaccine for the Quiet Year germ in just two days.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_puck|We still tested it carefully for weeks, because a medicine that was designed quickly still has to be proven safe.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_puck|When the vaccine was ready, every kid who got a shot got this sticker.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_puck|Each shot protected the person who got it, and the people around them too.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_puck|That is why the sticker says \u201cI helped Varrow,\u201d not \u201cI got a shot.\u201d":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
